WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.838
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:06.138 --> 00:00:09.443
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:16.157
Unit six: Economic Policy. Today you will compare with double comparatives: the more..., the more....

4
00:00:16.457 --> 00:00:19.005
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:23.211
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.411
Fiscal policy.

7
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:30.274
Fiscal policy means government decisions about taxes and spending.

8
00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:33.579
Fiscal policy can boost a weak economy.

9
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:37.161
Your turn. Say: Fiscal policy.

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.268
Fiscal policy.

11
00:00:42.017 --> 00:00:43.668
Monetary policy.

12
00:00:44.683 --> 00:00:49.469
Monetary policy means central bank decisions about money and interest rates.

13
00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:53.151
Monetary policy aims to control inflation.

14
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.820
Your turn. Say: Monetary policy.

15
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:01.341
Monetary policy.

16
00:01:02.083 --> 00:01:03.026
Subsidy.

17
00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:07.526
Subsidy means money the government gives to support an industry.

18
00:01:08.117 --> 00:01:10.511
A subsidy kept fuel prices low.

19
00:01:11.117 --> 00:01:13.955
Your turn. Say: Subsidy.

20
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:17.801
Subsidy.

21
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:19.679
Tax reform.

22
00:01:20.683 --> 00:01:24.123
Tax reform means a major change to how taxes are collected.

23
00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:26.832
Tax reform simplified the system.

24
00:01:27.417 --> 00:01:30.708
Your turn. Say: Tax reform.

25
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:34.835
Tax reform.

26
00:01:35.583 --> 00:01:36.863
Budget deficit.

27
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:41.475
Budget deficit means when a government spends more than it collects.

28
00:01:42.083 --> 00:01:44.292
A budget deficit means more borrowing.

29
00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:48.081
Your turn. Say: Budget deficit.

30
00:01:50.933 --> 00:01:52.213
Budget deficit.

31
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.125
Economic growth.

32
00:01:55.117 --> 00:01:59.160
Economic growth means an increase in the value of what a country produces.

33
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.168
Economic growth creates new jobs.

34
00:02:02.783 --> 00:02:05.981
Your turn. Say: Economic growth.

35
00:02:08.833 --> 00:02:10.357
Economic growth.

36
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:12.257
Regulation.

37
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.945
Regulation means an official rule made to control an activity.

38
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.921
New regulation protects consumers.

39
00:02:20.517 --> 00:02:22.890
Your turn. Say: Regulation.

40
00:02:25.733 --> 00:02:26.978
Regulation.

41
00:02:27.717 --> 00:02:28.915
Public spending.

42
00:02:29.917 --> 00:02:34.180
Public spending means money a government spends on services.

43
00:02:34.783 --> 00:02:36.911
Public spending on schools increased.

44
00:02:37.517 --> 00:02:40.308
Your turn. Say: Public spending.

45
00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:44.574
Public spending.

46
00:02:45.367 --> 00:02:49.273
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:50.117 --> 00:02:56.763
Visal, an editorial says 'The more the government spends, the larger the budget deficit becomes.' What is this structure?

48
00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:03.045
A double comparative: the + comparative, the + comparative. It shows that two things change together.

49
00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.906
Can I use it for policy?

50
00:03:07.017 --> 00:03:18.855
Of course. 'The lower the interest rate, the cheaper the loans.' Fiscal policy and monetary policy both involve these trade-offs.

51
00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.495
So how do tax reform and subsidies fit?

52
00:03:23.583 --> 00:03:31.588
The bigger the subsidy, the greater the cost to the budget. Economists use the structure to explain relationships clearly.

53
00:03:32.733 --> 00:03:34.582
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:35.417 --> 00:03:39.320
Grammar focus: Double comparatives: the more, the more.

55
00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:40.784
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:41.483 --> 00:03:44.795
The more the state spends, the larger the budget deficit becomes.

57
00:03:45.683 --> 00:03:48.786
The lower the interest rate, the cheaper loans become.

58
00:03:49.683 --> 00:03:53.041
The bigger the subsidy, the greater the cost to the budget.

59
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:01.923
Use the + comparative, the + comparative to show that two changes happen together. Keep the order subject + verb in each part.

60
00:04:02.683 --> 00:04:06.764
Be careful! Don't say: The lower the rate, cheaper the loan.

61
00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.710
Say: The lower the rate, the cheaper the loan.

62
00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.475
Both parts need 'the'.

63
00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:17.163
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:17.967 --> 00:04:19.595
Two Kinds of Policy.

65
00:04:19.895 --> 00:04:24.216
Governments manage the economy through fiscal policy and monetary policy.

66
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:32.343
Fiscal policy uses taxes and public spending, while monetary policy uses interest rates set by the central bank.

67
00:04:33.333 --> 00:04:38.653
The more a state spends, the larger its budget deficit becomes unless tax reform raises income.

68
00:04:39.667 --> 00:04:44.963
The bigger a subsidy is, the greater its cost, but economic growth may justify it.

69
00:04:45.967 --> 00:04:49.000
Careful regulation balances all of these goals.

70
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:53.858
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:54.717 --> 00:04:57.297
The more the state spends, the blank the deficit.

72
00:04:57.597 --> 00:05:01.146
A, larger. B, large. C, largest.

73
00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:06.630
The answer is A: larger.

74
00:05:07.783 --> 00:05:11.060
Decisions about taxes and spending are blank policy.

75
00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.932
A, fiscal. B, monetary. C, foreign.

76
00:05:18.467 --> 00:05:20.234
The answer is A: fiscal.

77
00:05:21.383 --> 00:05:24.660
Money given by the government to support an industry is a blank.

78
00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.194
A, subsidy. B, regulation. C, deficit.

79
00:05:32.733 --> 00:05:34.814
The answer is A: subsidy.

80
00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:37.706
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:38.006 --> 00:05:45.827
A, The lower the rate, cheaper the loans.. B, The lower the rate, the cheaper the loans.. C, The more low the rate, the cheap loans..

82
00:05:49.367 --> 00:05:52.481
The answer is B: The lower the rate, the cheaper the loans..

83
00:05:53.617 --> 00:05:57.892
According to the reading, monetary policy uses rates set by the…

84
00:05:58.192 --> 00:06:02.530
A, central bank. B, parliament. C, tax office.

85
00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:08.519
The answer is A: central bank.

86
00:06:09.733 --> 00:06:12.827
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:13.683 --> 00:06:17.285
What is the difference between fiscal policy and monetary policy?

88
00:06:26.083 --> 00:06:27.793
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:28.093 --> 00:06:54.374
Fiscal policy is controlled by the government and uses taxes and public spending to influence the economy. Monetary policy is controlled by the central bank and uses interest rates and the supply of money. In short, fiscal policy changes how much the government taxes and spends, whereas monetary policy changes how expensive it is to borrow.

90
00:06:54.674 --> 00:06:56.302
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:57.217 --> 00:07:02.388
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and double comparatives: the more, the more.

92
00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:07.907
Today's value is economic literacy. Understand how money and work shape people's lives.

93
00:07:08.207 --> 00:07:10.708
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:11.008 --> 00:07:13.858
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

